WEBVTT

NOTE Not knowing comes in different forms

1
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.191
Three people give the same answer: I do not know. One will roll a die.

2
00:00:10.191 --> 00:00:12.588
One holds a sealed envelope.

3
00:00:12.588 --> 00:00:17.467
One must guess the price of copper twenty years from now.

4
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:22.472
These people lack different things. What does each person not know?

5
00:00:22.472 --> 00:00:24.767
Why does that difference matter?

6
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:30.565
Uncertainty means that we do not know something. Two questions help us sort it.

7
00:00:30.565 --> 00:00:36.100
Does chance create the gap, or are facts missing? Can evidence support a number?

8
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:45.093
We will make four stops. First, chance or missing facts. Then, what we can measure.

9
00:00:45.093 --> 00:00:52.933
Next, unknown odds. Last, the range between certainty and total ignorance.

10
00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:55.781
Start with the die and the envelope.

11
00:00:55.781 --> 00:00:59.900
Both hide an answer, but they hide it in different ways.

12
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:06.517
No amount of study can reveal the next roll. Chance creates this uncertainty.

13
00:01:06.517 --> 00:01:09.933
Researchers call it aleatory uncertainty.

14
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:16.099
The number in the envelope is already fixed. Opening it gives us the missing fact.

15
00:01:16.099 --> 00:01:19.300
Researchers call this epistemic uncertainty.

16
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.300
Chance remains. Missing facts can be found.

17
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:28.260
We can find a missing fact, but we cannot remove chance.

18
00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:33.300
To give chance a number, we need many similar cases to count.

19
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:39.692
In 1921, Frank Knight used fire insurance as an example. One building may burn.

20
00:01:39.692 --> 00:01:45.100
Across many buildings, the insurer can measure how often fires occur.

21
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.589
A new business has no long record of close matches.

22
00:01:49.589 --> 00:01:56.233
Knight called the first case risk. He called this second case true uncertainty.

23
00:01:56.433 --> 00:02:02.797
In 1937, John Maynard Keynes made the same split. Roulette has known odds.

24
00:02:02.797 --> 00:02:08.300
A future war or the price of copper twenty years later does not.

25
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:15.020
For those long-range questions, Keynes wrote that no scientific basis supported a

26
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:20.333
calculation. His last sentence was plain. "We simply do not know."

27
00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:27.067
Honest odds need many similar cases. Without them, no sound number exists.

28
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:33.070
Knight and Keynes drew a sharp line between measurable risk and uncertainty.

29
00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:36.800
Daniel Ellsberg studied a case between those ends.

30
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.707
In 1961, Ellsberg described two urns. One holds fifty red and fifty black balls.

31
00:02:43.707 --> 00:02:48.067
The other holds one hundred balls in an unknown mix.

32
00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:54.145
Most people prefer the urn with the known mix, whichever color wins the bet.

33
00:02:54.145 --> 00:03:00.333
This case is called ambiguity. We know the possible results, but not their odds.

34
00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:02.786
Unknown odds call for a range.

35
00:03:02.786 --> 00:03:07.367
One exact number would claim more than the evidence supports.

36
00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:15.217
Chance, missing facts, weak groups, and unknown odds do not fit into four separate

37
00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:19.800
boxes. They lie along a scale of what we can know.

38
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.253
One end holds complete certainty.

39
00:03:22.253 --> 00:03:27.033
At the other end, we may not even know which questions we have missed.

40
00:03:27.333 --> 00:03:31.182
Before giving odds, place the forecast on this scale.

41
00:03:31.182 --> 00:03:34.667
Tell the audience what the evidence can support.

42
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.379
Chance remains, while missing facts can be found. Honest odds need similar cases.

43
00:03:41.379 --> 00:03:44.451
When the odds are unknown, use a range.

44
00:03:44.451 --> 00:03:49.333
First place the forecast on the scale, then choose the number.

45
00:03:49.533 --> 00:03:55.863
Now return to the three people. The die has known odds but an unknown result.

46
00:03:55.863 --> 00:03:59.563
The envelope holds a fixed fact we can learn.

47
00:03:59.563 --> 00:04:02.933
Copper's distant price has no sound odds.

48
00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:09.162
What does each person not know? One lacks a future result. One lacks a fact.

49
00:04:09.162 --> 00:04:15.667
One lacks sound odds. The difference decides whether a number can help or mislead.

50
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Once we know the kind of uncertainty, we can ask what a number can honestly say.
